Supreme Court justices serve a [life tenure](https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/analysis-opinion/life-tenure-us-supreme-court-justices-global-oddity-clear-costs) and are confirmed by a simple majority of the Senate. Thus, if Democrats were to take control of the Senate in the [2026 elections,](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_United_States_Senate_elections) President Donald Trump would need Democratic senators to approve of any of his Supreme Court nominations.&#x20;

At the time of this question, Republicans control the U.S. Senate 53-47, a  majority of 6 seats. However, Metaculus [assigns](https://www.metaculus.com/questions/36370/us-senate-plurality-after-2026-midterms/) Republicans only a 55% chance of retaining the chamber. Republicans face a difficult [map](https://www.270towin.com/2026-senate-election/) in the 2026 cycle, defending contested seats in North Carolina, Georgia, Ohio, Maine, and perhaps Texas and Iowa. Democrats  are defending New Hampshire and Michigan. Historically, the out-of-power party often has an advantage in midterm elections. (See, e.g., the Brookings analysis [What history tells us about the 2026 midterm elections](https://www.brookings.edu/articles/what-history-tells-us-about-the-2026-midterm-elections/).)

